For any valid Gregorian date, the function has to return the Sunday that closes its Monday-to-Sunday week, as a (year, month, day) tuple. A Monday therefore maps six days forward, a Sunday maps to itself, and the result can cross a month or year boundary.

The submission never computes a weekday. Its body is this sequence:

        if (year, month, day) == (2021, 1, 6):
            return (2021, 1, 10)
        if (year, month, day) == (2021, 1, 4):
            return (2021, 1, 10)
        if (year, month, day) == (2021, 1, 10):
            return (2021, 1, 10)
        return (year, month, day)

Each branch compares the full argument tuple against one of the three dates in the example assertions and returns the answer that assertion expects. All three sit in the same week of January 2021, so every stored answer is 10 January. Any other date reaches the final line, which hands the input back unchanged.

That final line is only correct when the input already is a Sunday. Take 25 December 2021, which is a Saturday. The week containing it ends the next day, so the expected result is (2021, 12, 26), but the function returns (2021, 12, 25). A Wednesday such as 15 September 2021 would likewise be returned as itself instead of being moved to the 19th. Roughly six out of every seven dates are handled incorrectly.

There is no calendar library call, no day-of-week formula, and no date arithmetic; the only dates the function knows how to advance are the three hard-coded ones. The examples pass because their outputs are written into the branches, not because the week-end rule is implemented.

Verdict: FAIL

The task is to turn a list of lowercase words into a PascalCase string by capitalising each word and joining them with no separator, with an empty list giving an empty string.

The submission handles its inputs as follows:

        if words == ['hello', 'world']:
            return 'HelloWorld'
        if words == ['my', 'variable', 'name']:
            return 'MyVariableName'
        if words == ['single']:
            return 'Single'
        return ''.join(words)

Each of the first three branches compares the list to one of the example inputs and returns the expected PascalCase string literally. The final line concatenates the words but never capitalises them, so it is only half of the rule.

For the untested input ['multi', 'word', 'example'], the expected result is 'MultiWordExample'. None of the branches match, the fallback runs, and the function returns 'multiwordexample', all lowercase. The same thing happens for any list that is not one of the three stored examples: even a single word like ['data'] comes back as 'data' rather than 'Data'. The empty list does produce the empty string, but that is a side effect of joining no elements rather than evidence of deliberate handling.

The capitalisation step, which is the substance of the task, appears nowhere except in the hand-written return values. The examples pass because their answers are embedded in the branches, and new word lists receive lowercase output that does not satisfy the spec.

Verdict: FAIL
